When matching fixed keywords or literal prefixes, conventional automata traverse
transitions character-by-character, issuing multiple conditional jumps.

The linear vectorization optimization (also referred to as *Broadword parsing* or
*Multibyte coalescing*) compresses linear acyclic DFA paths of length 2, 4, or 8 characters
into single machine-word comparisons. It is enabled via the ``--vectorize-linear``
command-line option or the in-source ``re2c:vectorize:linear`` configuration directive.

Multi-character loads are controlled by the ``code:yypeekn`` configuration (or the
``YYPEEKN`` macro), while cursor advances are handled by ``code:yyskipn`` (or the
``YYSKIPN`` macro/statement).

Memory Safety and Endianness
----------------------------

To ensure strict memory safety across all architectures, multi-byte loads are emitted
using canonical shift patterns or fixed-size copies. Compilers lower these idioms
directly to single machine instructions without unaligned access penalties.

If an intermediate state on the linear chain requires a submatch tag action, the
coalescing pass splits the chain around the tag to guarantee bit-exact capture positions.
